<section class="band-navy" id="how" aria-labelledby="how-title">
  <div class="wrap section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h2 id="how-title">Controls sit outside the model</h2>
      <p>AnswerHatch puts its controls in code that runs before and after the model, so a prompt cannot talk the system out of a rule.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="flow" style="--n:5">
      <li><h3>Crawl and register</h3><p>With your approval, and once you've proved control of the domain, your public pages are crawled. Each is recorded with its URL, fetch date and hash.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Screen the question</h3><p>A scope screen and a prompt-injection screen run before the model is called, and are built to turn away off-topic and adversarial requests.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Retrieve</h3><p>The question is matched only against the pages crawled from your site and held in the registry.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Cite or refuse</h3><p>An answer ships only with registered sources attached. With no source, it declines and gives the reason.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Test, then go live</h3><p>A test run on your own pages checks what it must answer and what it must refuse. You promote to production when you accept the results.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="boundary">
      <div>
        <h3>What the AI does</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Writes the answer in plain language from your registered pages.</li>
          <li>Matches your site's reading level, from plain English to statutory language.</li>
          <li>Answers spoken questions through the same pipeline as typed ones.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="never">
        <h3>What it is built not to do</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Answer without a source:</strong> no registered page means no answer.</li>
          <li><strong>Fill a gap:</strong> nothing plausible is composed to cover a question your site doesn't answer.</li>
          <li><strong>Pass screened requests to the model:</strong> requests the screens turn away are refused before the model is called.</li>
          <li><strong>Train on your content:</strong> models are not fine-tuned on your content or your visitors' questions.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="boundary-note">As stated on answerhatch.com.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band-mist" id="inside" aria-labelledby="inside-title">
  <div class="wrap section" data-acc-scope>
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Inside the product</p>
      <h2 id="inside-title">What you get, piece by piece</h2>
      <p>There is no feature gate between a small site and a large one. Every plan gets the whole product.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="acc-head">
      <h3>The product</h3>
      <button type="button" class="btn-text" data-expand-all hidden><span class="js-label">Expand all</span><span class="visually-hidden"> product sections</span></button>
    </div>
    <div class="points">
      <details id="answerhatch-widget">
        <summary><span class="acc-title">The widget</span><span class="hint">One script tag, keys bound to your domain</span></summary>
        <div class="acc-body">
          <p>The widget loads from AnswerHatch's content delivery network and renders on your pages. There is nothing to install on your server and no rebuild of your site.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Each key is issued against the domain you nominate. A key copied out of your page source does not serve answers anywhere else.</li>
            <li>Keys are stored hashed, not in plain text, and can be rotated from the portal.</li>
            <li>A consent and disclosure line appears when a chat starts, on every plan. It can't be switched off by accident.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details id="answerhatch-test-run">
        <summary><span class="acc-title">The test run</span><span class="hint">Evidence for acceptance, on your own pages</span></summary>
        <div class="acc-body">
          <p>Every trial includes a test run on your own pages. The results are yours to read in the portal, page by page, and to hand to procurement.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Pass list:</strong> the questions it must answer, each with the page it used. For example, the fees for a development application, where to report a missed bin, or opening hours.</li>
            <li><strong>Refusal list:</strong> the questions it must refuse, with the reason shown. For example, jailbreak attempts, off-topic steering and requests for figures your site doesn't publish.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details id="answerhatch-environments">
        <summary><span class="acc-title">Preprod and prod</span><span class="hint">Test before your visitors see it</span></summary>
        <div class="acc-body">
          <p>Every subscription runs a pre-production and a production environment, each with its own corpus, keys and bindings. You test a crawl in pre-production and promote it when you accept it.</p>
          <p>A crawl that fails verification produces a diagnostic. It never quietly becomes the live corpus.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details id="answerhatch-registry">
        <summary><span class="acc-title">The provenance registry</span><span class="hint">A record a compliance officer can ask for</span></summary>
        <div class="acc-body">
          <p>Crawled pages go into a registry of sources, derivations and decisions. The citations a visitor sees are a view of that registry, and your compliance officer can request the registry itself to audit exactly what the widget is allowed to say.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details id="answerhatch-voice">
        <summary><span class="acc-title">Voice</span><span class="hint">Ask out loud, hear the answer</span></summary>
        <div class="acc-body">
          <p>A visitor can speak the question and hear the answer read back, sources included. For a visitor who can't read the screen, the whole exchange can run by ear.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The Ask by voice button transcribes the question in the visitor's browser. Chrome and Edge use their vendor's speech service, and Safari transcribes on the device. AnswerHatch receives text, never audio.</li>
            <li>Every answer has a Listen button. A hands-free mode opens listening, reads the answer aloud and listens again for the next question.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details id="answerhatch-accessibility">
        <summary><span class="acc-title">Accessibility</span><span class="hint">Keyboard, screen reader and voice</span></summary>
        <div class="acc-body">
          <ul>
            <li>AnswerHatch states that the widget is built against the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1 AA.</li>
            <li>It can be operated by keyboard from end to end, with visible focus at every stop and no focus trap.</li>
            <li>Answers arrive in an ARIA live region, so screen readers announce them as they appear.</li>
            <li>It reads its own answers and works beside the screen reader a visitor already uses. It complements an accessible site rather than replacing the work of making one.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details id="answerhatch-government">
        <summary><span class="acc-title">For government and larger sites</span><span class="hint">Options quoted for your estate</span></summary>
        <div class="acc-body">
          <ul>
            <li>Hosting in a nominated region, with data residency.</li>
            <li>Identity federation and named user provisioning.</li>
            <li>A negotiated service level: 99.5% in business hours as standard, with round-the-clock cover as a priced option.</li>
            <li>An authenticated workspace, separate from the public widget, for derived products that were never published, such as forecasts, risk ratings and generated narrative.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section" id="data" aria-labelledby="data-title">
  <div class="section-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">What it processes</p>
    <h2 id="data-title">What AnswerHatch handles, and for how long</h2>
    <p>AnswerHatch is built for public content. Pages behind a login, case files and internal systems are out of scope for the public widget.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="What AnswerHatch processes" tabindex="0">
    <table class="ruled">
      <caption>What AnswerHatch processes</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col">What is processed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Your published pages</th><td>The public pages of the domain you nominate, crawled with your approval and stored with their URL, fetch date and hash.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Visitors' questions</th><td>The text of the question and the page it was asked from. Nothing else about the visitor is needed. Questions are kept for 30 days, so a fault can be diagnosed and a refusal reviewed, then deleted.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Voice</th><td>Speech is turned into text by the visitor's own browser. Only the text reaches AnswerHatch.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Your account</th><td>The contact email, the domains on the subscription and the usage counts the plan is measured against.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Operational logs</th><td>Request records used to run the service, enforce rate limits and diagnose faults.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="table-note">Questions can be found and deleted on request: give AnswerHatch the domain and roughly when the question was asked.</p>
</section>

<section class="band-mist" id="trust" aria-labelledby="trust-title">
  <div class="wrap section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Trust and security</p>
      <h2 id="trust-title">Where the data goes, stated plainly</h2>
      <p>These statements come from AnswerHatch's published security and privacy pages.</p>
    </div>
    <dl class="facts facts--icons">
      <div><dt class="ic ic--server">Hosting region</dt><dd>Customer traffic and data are processed in a single hosting region: Australia. Government and regulated buyers can nominate a region.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--cpu">Processing</dt><dd>On AnswerHatch-operated infrastructure. No third-party model API is in the path of an answer, so a question is never handed to an outside model provider.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--lock">Training</dt><dd>Models are not fine-tuned on your content or on your visitors' questions.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--clock">Retention</dt><dd>Visitor questions are kept for 30 days and then deleted.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--globe">Network</dt><dd>Customer traffic reaches the platform through a hardened edge behind Cloudflare, which carries DNS and the network path.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--check">Release control</dt><dd>A governance test suite gates every release, and refusal behaviour is part of the acceptance criteria.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--file">Security questionnaires</dt><dd>AnswerHatch answers government and regulated buyers' security questionnaires as written.</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="ic ic--file">Your privacy policy</dt><dd>Visitor questions are processed on your behalf. AnswerHatch provides wording you can paste into your own privacy policy.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section" id="questions" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
  <div data-acc-scope>
    <div class="acc-head">
      <h2 id="questions-title">Common questions</h2>
      <button type="button" class="btn-text" data-expand-all hidden><span class="js-label">Expand all</span><span class="visually-hidden"> questions</span></button>
    </div>
    <div class="points">
      <details id="q-ah-wrong"><summary><span class="acc-title">What if it says something wrong?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>Every answer carries the page it came from, so a reader can check it before acting on it. With no registered source, the widget declines and gives the reason. Try it on the demo: ask something in scope and it answers with links; ask something out of scope and it declines.</p></div></details>
      <details id="q-ah-model"><summary><span class="acc-title">Will our content end up in someone else's model?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>No. Questions are processed on machines AnswerHatch runs, no third-party model API is in the path, and models are not fine-tuned on your content or your visitors' questions.</p></div></details>
      <details id="q-ah-where"><summary><span class="acc-title">Where is the data processed?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>AnswerHatch states that data is processed in a single hosting region, Australia, and that government and regulated buyers can nominate a region.</p></div></details>
      <details id="q-ah-language"><summary><span class="acc-title">Our pages use formal or technical language. Will that work?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>Yes. The crawl measures how each page reads, from plain English for a council to statutory language for an agency, and answers are written to match. You run the widget against a pre-production copy of your site and accept it only when the tests pass on your own pages.</p></div></details>
      <details id="q-ah-it"><summary><span class="acc-title">Our IT and procurement teams will want evidence. What can we give them?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>The test-run results from your own domain, the provenance registry of every page the widget may answer from, and written answers to your security questionnaire.</p></div></details>
      <details id="q-ah-price"><summary><span class="acc-title">How is it priced?</span></summary><div class="acc-body"><p>After the free trial, pricing is quoted for your site, based on the pages you publish and the domains the widget runs on. You get a yearly figure in writing. Answers are fair use, with no per-answer meter and no credit balance to run down.</p></div></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

